      'Seven days is the sweet spot for Tenerife: long enough to see the volcano, the whales, the beaches and the wild north without rushing. This itinerary is built around a base in the south (Playa Las Americas, Los Cristianos or Costa Adeje), where most visitors stay, with day trips out across the island. Shuffle the days to match the weather.',
      'Day 1 — Settle in and the south coast. Ease into island time on the beaches of Los Cristianos and Costa Adeje, walk the promenade, and get your bearings. In the evening, kick off the trip with something memorable: a self-guided <a href="/en/escape-game">outdoor escape game</a> in a nearby town.',
      'Day 2 — Mount Teide. Spain\'s highest peak and a UNESCO World Heritage Site is the island\'s must-do. Drive up through the pine forest into the lunar Teide National Park, ride the <a href="/en/activities/teide-cable-car-tour">cable car</a> toward the summit, and if you can, stay for a stargazing tour — the skies here are among the clearest on Earth.',
      'Day 3 — Whales and the water. The south-west is one of the world\'s best spots for year-round whale and dolphin watching. Take a <a href="/en/activities/whale-watching-tenerife">whale-watching boat trip</a> or a <a href="/en/activities/catamaran-sunset-cruise-tenerife">catamaran cruise</a> with a swim stop, and spend the afternoon on the beach.',
      'Day 4 — The wild north. Swap the resorts for green Tenerife: the colonial streets of La Orotava and Puerto de la Cruz, the historic town of La Laguna, and the dramatic Anaga mountains with their laurel forest and black-sand beaches. This is the island most visitors never see — and the backdrop of our outdoor <a href="/en/escape-game">escape games</a>, which turn these towns into the board.',
