      'When the sun goes down, the south of Tenerife wakes up. The resorts around Playa de las Americas and Costa Adeje have one of the liveliest after-dark scenes in the Canary Islands — everything from quiet cocktail terraces to full-throttle clubbing — and it runs all year thanks to the warm evenings. Here is how to do a night out in Tenerife.',
      'The epicentre is the Veronicas strip in Playa de las Americas: a dense cluster of bars and clubs that is the heart of the party scene and a magnet for stag and hen groups. It is loud, cheap and unpretentious — exactly what some nights call for. For something more relaxed, the marinas and promenades of Costa Adeje and Los Cristianos have stylish cocktail bars and restaurants with sea views.',
      'Beyond the bars, the south has dinner shows, flamenco, live music and beach clubs that drift from afternoon into night. For a classic Canarian evening, a <a href="/en/activities/wine-tasting-tenerife">wine tasting</a> in the Tacoronte hills or a dinner-and-show night makes a great change of pace. Browse the evening options on our <a href="/en/activities">things to do in Tenerife</a> page.',
      'Here is a local tip most visitors miss: the best nights out do not start in a bar. Warm up the group with an activity first. A self-guided <a href="/en/escape-game">outdoor escape game</a> — exploring the heart of Playa Las Americas on your phone — breaks the ice, gets everyone competitive and sets the tone before you hit the strip. It is why so many groups book it as the opener.',
      'For groups and celebrations, this is the formula. Stag dos, hen parties and birthdays build the night around a shared activity, then roll into dinner and the bars. If that is you, our <a href="/en/stag-hen-party-tenerife">group celebrations page</a> sorts the whole thing — prosecco on arrival, matching tees and a leaderboard with the guest of honour\'s name.',
